  "content": {
    "title": "Iraq secure final spot in 2026 World Cup with Aymen Hussein winner in 2-1 win over Bolivia",
    "text": "© Rodrigo Oropeza/Getty ImagesAymen Hussein of Iraq.Iraq and Bolivia clashed on Tuesday, March 31, at Estadio BBVA in Monterrey, Mexico, in the FIFA international playoff. With Aymen Hussein scoring the decisive goal, the Asian side claimed the last ticket to the 2026 World Cup.The game got off to a fast start for Iraq, who broke the deadlock in the 10th minute through a well-delivered corner from Amir Al-Ammari that Ali Al-Hamadi headed home inside the box. Bolivia leveled in the 38th minute, just as they had against Suriname, with Moises Paniagua controlling a shot from Ramiro Vaca and finishing clinically inside the box.Iraq struck again early in the second half, with substitute Marko Farji making an instant impact just one minute after coming on, delivering the cross that allowed Hussein to restore the lead in the 53rd minute. From that point, La Verde resorted to long-range efforts and constant crosses, but a well-organized Iraqi low block proved too difficult to break down.With their place secured, Iraq have been slotted into Group I at the 2026 World Cup alongside France, Norway and Senegal. The Lions of Mesopotamia will open their campaign against Erling Haaland’s Norway, before facing Kylian Mbappe’s Les Bleus on Matchday 2 and rounding out the group stage against Sadio Mané’s Lions of Teranga, in what will be only their second World Cup appearance since Mexico 1986. 01:20AMThank you for joining us!That’s the end of our live blog, with Iraq securing the last ticket for the 2026 World Cup after a 2-1 win against Bolivia. See you next time!Iraq hold on to claim a 2-1 victory over Bolivia, securing the final ticket to the 2026 World Cup. 12:59AM97' – Massive block by Jasim (2-1)After a series of rebounds fell to Paniagua inside the box, Jasim produced an outstanding block to deny what could have been Bolivia's equalizer. 12:56AM94' – Great save by Basil (2-1)Nava tried a narrow shot, and even though it was going out, Basil pulled out a great save. 12:53AM90' – Nine more minutes! (2-1)Nine minutes of stoppage time have been added. 12:52AM90' – Substitution in Bolivia (2-1)Robson Matheus out, Enzo Monteiro in. 12:52AM90' – Yellow card for Bolivia (2-1)After tackling down Jasim who was leading a counterattack, Lucas Macazaga sees the yellow card. 12:46AM84' – Another substitution in Iraq (2-1)As his fifth substitution, coach Arnold withdraws Ibrahim Bayesh from the field, bringing on Ali Jasim in his place. 12:44AM82' – Iraq misses clear chance! (2-1)Iraq caught Bolivia on the counter, with Farji delivering a cross to Mohanad Ali, who got a first-time effort away but sent it well over the bar and into the stands. 12:42AM80' – Yellow card for Iraq (2-1)Marko Farji becomes the latest Iraqi player to be cautioned after another cynical foul. 12:38AM76' – Yellow card for Iraq (2-1)After a rough challenge over Macazaga, Zaid Ismail sees the yellow card. 12:37AM75' – Shot easy for Basil (2-1)With Iraq sitting deep and space hard to come by, Nava tried his luck from distance, but the effort was comfortable for Basil to gather. 12:34AM71' – Yellow card for Bolivia (2-1)Efrain Morales is shown the first yellow card of the game for Bolivia after catching Marko Farji in the face with an elbow during an aerial duel. 12:33AM71' – Game resumes with other Bolivia substitution (2-1)As the cooling break concluded, Ramiro Vaca is replaced by Fernando Nava. 12:31AM68' – Cooling break (2-1)The cooling break respective to the second half has started. 12:29AM67' – First substitution in Bolivia (2-1)Forced out of the field due to injury, Diego Medina is replaced by Lucas Macazaga. 12:28AM67' – Double substitution in Iraq (2-1)Iraq manager Arnold shakes things up, withdrawing both strikers as Aymen Hussein and Ali Al-Hamadi make way for Mohanad Ali and Ali Jasim. 12:27AM65' – Bolivia medical staff jump on the field to assist Medina (2-1)Diego Medina, who was in doubt for the clash against Iraq, drops to the ground after a discomfort in his left leg. 12:25AM63' – Bolivia struggling to make an impact in the air (2-1)Despite a succession of corners and high balls into Iraq's box, Bolivia have been unable to make their aerial presence count. 12:20AM58' – Iraq back in a low block after the goal (2-1)Reverting to their first-half approach, Iraq have dropped deep once again, leaving Bolivia to search for an equalizer through crosses and long-range efforts. 12:15AM53' – GOOOOOOOOAAAAAAAAAAL FOR IRAQ!!! (2-1)After heading down a long ball, Aymen Hussein received a well-timed cross from Marko Farji and finished first time to put Iraq back in front. 12:14AM52' – Substitutions in Iraq (1-1)Youssef Amyn is stretchered off the field after pulling up with the injury, with Marko Farji coming on as his replacement. Zaid Ismail also enters the fray, taking the place of Aimar Sher. 12:11AM49' – Game stopped due to injury (1-1)Youssef Amyn went down on the pitch after appearing to feel discomfort in his left hamstring following a backheel in the middle of the field. 12:07AMSecond half underway!Referee Ivan Barton blows the whistle, and the second half is underway!Iraq and Bolivia are tied 1-1 after the first 45 minutes, with Ali Al Hamadi and Moises Paniagua as the scorers. 11:52PM52' – Missed chance by Bolivia (1-1)Luis Haquin got on the end of a corner kick and directed a header toward goal, coming agonizingly close to giving Bolivia the lead. 11:46PM45' – Five more minutes (1-1)Five minutes of stoppage time have been added. 11:44PM44' – Game stopped due to injury (1-1)After capturing Vaca's free kick, Iraq goalkeeper Basil dropped to the ground appearing to have picked up an injury, consequently stopping the game. 11:43PM43' – Bolivia grow after the equalizer (1-1)Energized by Paniagua's goal, Bolivia have continued to push for a winner, with Vaca winning a free kick after a handball was called following another long-range attempt. 11:38PM38' – GOOOOOOOOOAAAAAAAAAAL FOR BOLIVIA!! (1-1)After bringing down what initially appeared to be a long-range effort from Vaca inside the box, Moises Paniagua showed composure to place a clinical finish into the far corner and level the scores. 11:33PM32' – Bolivia adds another shot to the count (1-0)Unable to break Iraq's defense, Vaca tried a long shot that went straight to Basil's hands. 11:32PM32' – Discussion over penalty (1-0)Iraq and Bolivia players start discussing on the box over an unawarded penalty to the South American side. 11:31PM31' – Nerves and imprecision linger over the game (1-0)With neither side willing to make a mistake, both Iraq and Bolivia are playing it safe, giving the ball away cheaply while struggling to create any clear-cut chances. 11:26PM26' – Game resumes (1-0)After three minutes, the game between Iraq and Bolivia resumes. 11:24PM23' – Cooling break (1-0)Following the same regulations that will be in place at the 2026 World Cup, the mandatory cooling break for the first half has been called. 11:20PM19' – First yellow card of the game (1-0)Ibrahim Bayesh picks up the first yellow card of the game after a late challenge on Matheus. 11:15PM15' – Bolivia pushing for the equalizer (1-0)Following the goal, Iraq have dropped deeper, ceding possession and giving Bolivia the space to push forward in search of an equalizer. 11:10PM10' – GOOOOOOOAAAAAAAAAAL FOR IRAQ!! (1-0)Iraq made the most of the ensuing corner, delivering the ball into the box for Ali Al-Hamadi to get on the end of it and head home the opener. 11:08PM9' – Viscarra flies to avoid the opener (0-0)After Aimar Sher was fouled just outside the box, Amir Al-Ammari stepped up and unleashed a stunning strike, only to be denied by an equally brilliant save from Vizcarra to keep the scores level. 11:05PM4' – Bolivia also tries from distance (0-0)Villamil spotted goalkeeper Basil off his line, but his ambitious effort from well beyond the halfway line was comfortable enough for the keeper to collect. 11:03PM3' – Iraq's first shot goes up (0-0)After a free kick played short, Ibrahim Bayesh tried from distance but his shot went far up the goal.Referee Ivan Barton blows the whistle, and the game between Iraq and Bolivia is underway.` 10:55PMWho is the referee for Iraq vs. Bolivia?The referee selected for Tuesday's match between Iraq and Bolivia is Ivan Barton, a 35-year-old official from El Salvador with extensive experience officiating CONCACAF fixtures.Match officials:Referee: Ivan Barton 1st Assistant: David Morán 2nd Assistant: Henri Pupiro Fourth Official: Said Martinez VAR: Tatiana Guzmán VAR assistant: Guillermo Pacheco 10:40PMBolivia's lineup confirmed!Head coach Oscar Villegas confirmed the starting lineup to break Bolivia's streak of 32 years without participating in the World Cup (4-3-3): Guillermo Vizcarra; Diego Medina, Luis Haquin, Efrain Morales, Roberto Fernandez; Robson Matheus, Ramiro Vaca, Gabriel Villamil; Miguel Terceros, Moises Paniagua, Juan Godoy. 10:25PMIraq's starting XI confirmed!Australian head coach Graham Arnold confirmed the elevn players that'll be looking for the World Cup ticket from the start (4-4-2): Amald Basil; Hussein Ali, Zaid Tahseen, Akam Hashim, Merchas Doski; Ibrahim Bayesh, Aimar Sher, Amir Al Amari, Youssef Amin; Aymen Hussein, Ali Al Hamad. 10:10PMKickoff time and how to watchThe game between Iraq and Bolivia will kick off at 23 PM (ET).You can watch the international friendly live on Fubo, Peacock Premium, Fox Sports 1, FOX One and Telemundo Deportes Ahora. 09:55PMIraq and Bolivia clash in the FIFA international 2026 World Cup QualifiersWelcome to our live blog for the final of the FIFA International playoff between Iraq and Bolivia. Stay tuned for key updates and live minute-by-minute coverage!200+ Channels & Local SportsPrice: Plans starting at $14.99/mo (Latino)Watch Ligue 1, Copa Libertadores & World Cup QualifiersEvery MLS Match in One PlacePrice: $12.99/mo (Now included with standard subscription)Watch every MLS regular season game, Playoffs & Leagues CupMany Sports & ESPN OriginalsPrice: $11.99/mo (or ESPN Unlimited for $29.99/mo)Features LaLiga, Bundesliga, FA Cup & NWSL2,000+ Soccer Games Per YearPrice: Starting at $8.99/moFeatures Champions League, Serie A & Europa LeagueHome of the Premier LeaguePrice: Starting at $10.99/mo175+ Exclusive EPL matches per seasonBoliviaIraqWorld CupWorld Cup Qualifiers",
